<section>
    <p>When I started working as tech support back in 2009, there was a phrase that I often heard from various people:</p>
    <blockquote>We don't work with assumptions.</blockquote>
    <p>I strongly believe that to be incorrect. Assumptions are the salt (and soul) of this job. That's something that I taught others as well:</p>
    <blockquote>Accept the fact that you don't know anything.</blockquote>
    <p>Not because it may sound cool, but because it's the truth. When you start, you don't know anything about the product you're supporting. You learn about it as you work, but at the same time people are changing it. Most likely you don't know who, why, how, when and what is being changed. Even after years of supporting the product, you'll be finding things that are new. Or you can just forget stuff, unless you have
        <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyperthymesia" target="_blank">hyperthymesia</a>.
    </p>
    <p>Even with perfect recollection you
        <em>have</em> to rely on whatever information given to you, and that information is given to you by people. They're often not familiar with the product or certain parts of it, aren't technical, are in a rush, tired, angry or even a mix of those and more. The issue reporter may be missing some minor detail, because it's not obvious to them, and because of that - they may not be sharing the full picture of what's going on. Besides, remember, that
    </p>
    <img crossorigin="anonymous" loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="w50pc middle block gallery_zoom" src="/assets/images/supops/memes/everybody_lies.avif" alt="Everybody lies" width="500" height="328">
    <p>Maybe not intentionally, but that's beside the point. The point is that we
        <em>do</em> work with assumptions on a daily basis. The important thing is to
        <em>validate</em> those assumptions whenever you can. Ask a question, request a screenshot, read documentation, peek into the code, test (ideally on a test environment). Only once you validate as many assumptions, as possible for a particular case, can you make weighed, (hopefully) correct and long-lasting decisions.
    </p>
    <p>That is why SupOps is built on <em>FACTS</em>.</p>
    <p>Yes,
        <em>FACTS</em>, all caps. Because it's not just literal facts. A good concept (or even ideology) is built on principles (or "pillars") that you try to uphold, and FACTS are like a Megazord from Power Rangers: a principle made out of 5 other core principles. This also shows how principles can support each other in some ways, because a singular good practice can match multiple principles.
    </p>
    <img crossorigin="anonymous" loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="w50pc middle block gallery_zoom" src="/assets/images/supops/memes/megazord.avif" alt="Megazord" width="533" height="300">
